  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES imagery captures the remnants of two \nconvective clusters that developed across ND/northern MN and \nNE/IA overnight. Our forecast area remained dry between these \nclusters, though we have observed expansive mid-to-high clouds \nassociated with convective debris. Visible satellite loop \ndisplays clouds thinning in the wake of the morning convection, \nwhich should allow for some more sun to filter through this \nafternoon and support forecast highs in the upper 70s to lower \n80s. \n\nLooking upstream, a closed upper-low is captured on water vapor\nimagery over central Saskatchewan. The feature is forecast to \nmove eastward across Manitoba tonight and then pivot \nsoutheastward towards Lake Superior heading into Wednesday. \nAssociated surface low pressure will follow a similar track, \nwhich will send a cold front from west to east across the Upper \nMidwest. Winds have turned southerly ahead of the incoming \nsystem, which will support both temperature and moisture \nadvection. Sensibly, it will be a mild night with lows in the \nmid 60s across most of the forecast area (slightly cooler into \nwestern WI) and warmer temperatures (mid 80s) follow for \nTuesday's highs. In addition, dew points will be on the rise \nover the next 24-hours, which will yield sufficient instability \nfor thunderstorms ahead of the front. We're looking at two \npotential rounds of convection tied to the frontal passage: 1) \nLate tonight into Tuesday morning, mainly across western and \ncentral MN. 2) Tuesday afternoon and evening, generally \ndeveloping across far eastern MN (I-35) and moving east into \nwestern WI.\n\n1) Late Tonight - Tuesday Morning...Thunderstorms are forecast \nto develop across the Dakotas this afternoon and progress \neastward into northwestern MN tonight. The storms are forecast \nto outrun a plume of stronger instability across the Dakotas, \nwhich will result in a weakening trend by the time they advance \ninto northwestern portions of the MPX forecast area. Forecast \nsoundings at AXN and STC highlight this idea, with skinny \nelevated instability located above very stable low-levels. SPC \nhas maintained a Slight Risk across portions of eastern ND/SD \nand far NW MN, where a more favorable environment for deeper \nconvection exists. Locally, communities across far western MN \nare under a Marginal Risk (1/5) for the potential for an \nisolated instance of damaging wind or hail as the storms \napproach after 10 PM. This potential quickly drops-off as storms\nmove east across central MN after midnight.\n\n2) Tuesday Afternoon/Evening...Weakening showers and isolated \nthunderstorms will likely be ongoing Tuesday morning across \nportions of the area. While there is a level of uncertainty \nregarding atmospheric recovery following the morning \nprecipitation/clouds, ongoing southwesterly flow ahead of the \ntrue surface front should aid in destabilization and yield a \nnarrow plume of CAPEs on the order of ~1000-2000 J/kg. In \naddition to the instability, a jetstreak south of the upper-low \nwill support 30-35kts of effective shear, supportive of deeper \nconvection. One of the forecast challenges over the past few \ndays has narrowing down areal and temporal uncertainties with \nregards to convective initiation. While a few \"western\" \nsolutions suggest afternoon CI across portions of central to \nsouthern MN, the latest HREF/REFS ensemble consensus follows \ntrends in ML guidance that favors CI across eastern MN. With \nthis in mind, we'll look for locations along and east of I-35 as\nhaving the best chance for strong to severe convection Tuesday \nafternoon and evening. Deeper analysis shows an environment \nfavorable for a few isolated supercell structures across western\nWI, where elongated low-level curvature is present in \nhodographs between RNH/EAU. SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk \n(1/5) across much of the forecast area, which is reasonable \ngiven the overall modest convective setup with uncertainties \nregarding destabilization. Should surface based convection \nrealize the potential environment, damaging winds, large hail, \nand a tornado or two would be possible. \n\nConvection exits the forecast area by 10 PM Tuesday evening and\nwill be followed by a mostly quiet second half of the work \nweek. The core of the upper-low will continue to dig \nsoutheastward into the Great Lakes Wednesday, which in turn will\nsupport breezy northwesterly flow across the Upper Midwest. \nWe'll run with a dry forecast for now, however we eventually may\nneed to include isolated to scattered shower chances within the\nmid-level CAA regime Wednesday afternoon. Surface high pressure\nbuilds east Thursday and Friday and will bring a pleasant end \nto the work week with highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s along \nwith comfortable dew points. The extended forecast features the \nreturn of a more active pattern as a series of Pacific \nshortwaves traverse the northern periphery of the Four Corners \nridge that has dominated the ",
